\description{
Tests on the equality of proportions using
large-sample statistics. It tests that a sample has the same proportion
within two independent groups or two samples have the same proportion.
Both variables must be binary; the second level of each variable is
treated as the success level. Character, logical and 0/1 encodings are
converted to a factor with a shared success level before the groups are
tabulated.
}
\details{
Supported inputs: finite data with absolute value \code{<= 1e150}
and sample sizes \code{<= 1e15}. In \code{ifr_ts_prop_calc()}, sample sizes
must be positive integers no greater than \code{1e15} and proportions must
be finite values in \code{[0, 1]}. Observations with missing values are
removed; each sample must retain at least one observation.
All three entry points reject samples whose proportions are both zero or
both one, because the pooled z test then has zero standard error and is
undefined. A single boundary proportion or opposite boundary proportions
are allowed.

The pooled standard error is computed with the direct pooled-variance
expression for ordinary proportions and with a factored square root when
that product would underflow, so probabilities near zero and near one remain
finite rather than producing an infinite z. The z statistic is stored to
three decimal places and unrounded in \code{z_raw}. p-values are stored to
three decimal places and unrounded in \code{sig_raw}; they are printed as
bounds when they are below the display resolution.
}
